[#:seon.task{:name "refusal-offending-arguments-held-by-reference"
             :intent "An invalid-input refusal carries the offending arguments as values in its ex-data (:seon.error/offending [unit]); anything that prints or stores the diagnostic can realize them — a test failure pr-str'd an endless lazy seq and OOM'd default on 2026-09-27 (fixed at the runner in b703e5ae9 by storing through admission's bound, which still realizes up to 8 MiB). Hold offending arguments by reference (result handle / blob digest / bounded admitted value) at the producer so no consumer can realize more than what is shown. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/landing/large-value-feedback-opus-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-refusal-omits-the-data-that-explains-it"
             :intent "A refusal reaches the agent as its message and a two-frame stack; the evidence its producer decided on is not in the value. Dogfood loop 2026-09-28: request-merge! refused 'Cannot interpret `seon.sci.eval/acquire-program!`: ... calls a definition this branch changes' without naming which changed definition or the call path from the refused declaration to it; finding that (`seon.render.value/tree`, 419 transitive callers, 13 host-bound) took a second evaluation written by hand. A producer's refusal carries the data it decided on (here the changed definitions reached and one path to each), by reference when large, so the reply explains itself and no refusal needs digging. Read `seon.error.refusal/diagnostic` and `docs/research/agent-platform/dogfood-loop-2026-09-28.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "hand-built-error-maps-remain-across-the-namespaces"
             :intent "Error sweep: batch 1 converted config (0a75ca319 detection only), the test runner (32ebd64f6) and cluster.agent (d0961f05e); hand-built error maps and per-namespace copies of the error helpers (error?, error, let-ok) remain: ai.clj:635, plan-era copies, render/transcript.clj:744, the 44 (contains? x `:seon.error/at`) checks, the 15 three-key checks and the 17 debt: comments. Rule: every error is built by ONE constructor validated against its declared alternative where it is made. Done when each remaining namespace, one per commit, rewrites its error? ladders first, then literal maps grouped by enclosing defn with the alternative from its output union (boot-time producers checked: error refuses without a projection), and the census counts fall to zero for it. Read `seon.error.refusal/error?`, `seon.error/render-faults-ai` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/root-cause-census--report.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "errors-capture-whole-stack-traces"
             :intent "Errors and caller lookups capture or walk whole stack traces: error.refusal stack-frames and current-frames, instrument caller-frame, schema fallback-caller, fs caller-frame and Throwable->map stored whole as :seon.error/cause or :seon.error/offending (test runner result-read-error, sci kernel triage, sci admit, operator launch forms). Owner 2026-10-07: 10k-line traces are useless; Seon's own error carries the producer's source location and the cause chain (class, message, ex-data per link) as data, never a trace. Inventory class stack-trace, 15 sites: `seon.error/refusal`.clj 4, script/operator.clj 2, instrument.clj 2, schema.clj 2, script/operator_outcome_test.clj 1, fs.clj 1, sci/admit.clj 1, sci/kernel.clj 1, test/runner.clj 1. Done when no error value or stored fact holds a stack trace or Throwable->map, caller attribution reads one frame by StackWalker limit, and seon.error.refusal-test asserts a 5,000-deep recursion's error holds its chain and location with no frame list. Read `seon.error.refusal/chain`, `seon.instrument/caller-frame`, `seon.schema/fallback-caller`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/no-print-hash--notes.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/no-print-hash--print-sites.tsv`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "identity-folding-assumes-stable-projectors",
            :intent
            "3071fef19 memoizes completed nodes although post-order and referenced admit arbitrary callables. A stateful projector under a shared map returns parent [{:x 1} {:x 2}] versus change [{:x 1} {:x 1}]. Choose and declare once-per-identity semantics or preserve per-occurrence projection; prove through references with a registered projector. Shared 300 x 10000 depth: 11336.9 ms / 7249546240 allocated bytes parent, 93.8 ms / 27523840 change in SCI copies. Success tests must include the chosen mutable-world semantics. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/growth/review-growth-codex-notes.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "the-error-data-walk-allocates-a-kilobyte-per-collection-node",
            :intent
            "`seon.error.refusal/referenced` allocates about 1.5 KB per collection node it walks, even when nothing is rebuilt: `(seon.error.refusal/references nil (mapv vector (range 1000000)))` takes 0.9-1.3 s and allocates 1.5 GB (compiled, unarmed, throwaway JVM). The cost is the explicit stack's node maps, `entries` tuples (`map-indexed vector`) and one armed `entries` call per node on default. Correct: a walk that rebuilds nothing allocates a small constant per node (reuse one mutable frame per stack level, iterate members without tuples), so error data the size of an agent's value is referenced well under one second. Proof: a profile probe of the 1M-vector walk under 300 ms / 200 MB."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-interpreted-callback-is-blamed-on-the-compiled-function-calling-it"
             :intent "An agent's own contract mistake made inside an interpreted closure that a compiled function calls is recorded as Seon's fault. `(seon.db/with-branch {} #(seon.db/transact! nil []))` evaluated by an agent is refused by the armed `seon.db/transact!` (argument 0 nil), and `seon.error.refusal/calling-definition` names the compiled `seon.db/with-branch` as `:seon.error/caller` and `:seon.error/producer`, so the evaluation boundary records an open fault against first-party code and default's health turns faulted (what-broke, signature af537e5b on default, 2026-10-09). The blame is the interpreted closure's: `seon.error.refusal/producer` must name the agent's interpreted frame (SCI's stacktrace) when an interpreted frame sits between the refused call and the first compiled first-party frame, so the mistake is only delivered on the form, never recorded. Second reproduction, no closure (chain5-findings on default 6aca2052, 2026-10-10): the agent form `(my.program/search \"match-row\" {})` (database argument a string) was refused at argument 0 and root received the error as message 6453ce66c0c7, `:seon.instrument/caller` \"seon.sci.eval/lazy-root (eval.clj:2867)\": `calling-definition` stops at the first-party frame `seon.sci.eval/lazy-root`, which is the evaluator and the agent's form, not authored caller code. Frames of `seon.sci.eval` belong to the interpreter like `sci.*` ones. The same class explains root's unread invocation-layer notices from repl-only-3, chain2-forgive-2 and message-wakes. Third reproduction, no closure and a returning `my.*` tool (open-faults-evening, row 3, default f7c730911): agent chain-2-probe passed the keyword `:agent-8f004cfa2806` positionally to `my.agent/retire-branch!`, which declares `:my.agent/branch-request` (a map); the invalid-input refusal (`:seon.instrument/arm :input`, layer `:seon.instrument/invocation`) is recorded as open error signature 117f391d785b and `seon.problems/open-faults` marks default `:faulted`. AGENTS.md: an agent's own mistake is only delivered. The recording decision reads `seon.error.refusal/producer` and `seon.error.refusal/attributed`: when the refused call's caller is the agent's form (the interpreter's frames, `seon.sci.eval/lazy-root` included, are no authored caller), the refusal is delivered on the form and nothing is recorded. Red proof: `seon.cluster.evaluate-source-test/an-agents-positional-call-of-a-my-tool-records-no-fault` evaluates `(my.agent/retire-branch! :some-branch)` through `with-started-agent`'s `code` and asserts the evaluation carries `:seon.cluster.eval/error` while `(seon.problems/open-faults (db/db connection))` is empty (HEAD: one open fault whose `:seon.error/fact` names `my.agent/retire-branch!`). Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-09/what-broke.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/open-faults-evening.md`."}]
